<sn><md>#### 1. Understanding the Four States in Git

Before learning undo commands, understand the basic Git workflow:

```text
Working Directory
       │
       │ git add
       ▼
Staging Area
       │
       │ git commit
       ▼
Local Repository
       │
       │ git push
       ▼
Remote Repository
```

For example:

```text
file.php
   │
   ├── Modified but unstaged
   │
   ├── Staged
   │
   ├── Committed locally
   │
   └── Pushed to GitHub
```

Each state requires a different undo technique.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 2. Check What You Need to Undo

Always start with:

```bash
git status
```

For example:

```text
On branch main

Changes not staged for commit:
  modified: index.php

Changes to be committed:
  modified: config.php

Untracked files:
  test.php
```

This tells you exactly where your changes are.

You can also inspect differences:

```bash
git diff
```

Shows **unstaged changes**.

```bash
git diff --staged
```

Shows **staged changes**.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 3. Undo Local Changes in a File

Suppose you modified:

```text
index.php
```

but have **not committed** the changes.

Check:

```bash
git status
```

If you want to completely discard the changes:

```bash
git restore index.php
```

The file returns to the version from the latest commit.

**Example**

Before:

```text
Hello World
```

You modify it to:

```text
Hello Git
```

Then:

```bash
git restore index.php
```

It becomes:

```text
Hello World
```

**Important**

This permanently discards the uncommitted changes in that file.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 4. Undo All Unstaged Local Changes

If you modified many files and want to discard **all unstaged changes**:

```bash
git restore .
```

This restores all tracked files to their committed state.

For example:

```text
modified: index.php
modified: style.css
modified: script.js
```

Run:

```bash
git restore .
```

All three files return to their last committed versions.

**Warning**

This can permanently destroy your uncommitted work.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 5. Undo a File That Was Staged

Suppose:

```bash
git add index.php
```

Now:

```bash
git status
```

shows:

```text
Changes to be committed:
    modified: index.php
```

You want to **remove it from staging**, but keep the modifications.

Use:

```bash
git restore --staged index.php
```

Now:

```text
Staging Area
     ↓
removed

Working Directory
     ↓
changes remain
```

So:

```bash
git restore --staged index.php
```

does **not** delete your changes.

It only removes the file from the staging area.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 6. Unstage All Files

If you staged several files:

```bash
git add .
```

and want to unstage everything:

```bash
git restore --staged .
```

Your changes remain in the working directory.

For example:

```text
Before:

Working Directory → modified
Staging Area       → modified
```

After:

```bash
git restore --staged .
```

you get:

```text
Working Directory → modified
Staging Area       → clean
```

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 7. Unstage and Discard the Changes

If you want to both:

1. remove a file from staging, and
2. delete its modifications

you can use:

```bash
git restore --staged index.php
git restore index.php
```

Or, depending on your Git version, you can restore both working tree and index from `HEAD`:

```bash
git restore --source=HEAD --staged --worktree index.php
```

This returns the file completely to its latest committed state.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 8. Undo an Untracked File

Suppose you created:

```text
test.php
```

but never ran `git add`.

`git status` shows:

```text
Untracked files:
    test.php
```

Git does not track this file yet.

You can delete it normally:

```bash
rm test.php
```

On Windows CMD:

```cmd
del test.php
```

If you want Git to remove untracked files:

```bash
git clean
```

First preview what would be deleted:

```bash
git clean -n
```

Then actually delete:

```bash
git clean -f
```

For untracked directories too:

```bash
git clean -fd
```

**Very important**

`git clean` can permanently delete untracked files.

Always use:

```bash
git clean -n
```

first.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 9. Undo the Last Commit but Keep the Changes

Suppose you made:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Update UI"
```

Then realize:

&gt; I committed too early. I want to modify the files more.

Use:

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

The commit disappears, but the changes remain **staged**.

```text
Before:

Working Directory
       ↓
Staging Area
       ↓
Commit A
```

After:

```text
Working Directory
       ↓
Staging Area
       ↓
HEAD
```

The changes are still staged.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 10. Undo the Last Commit and Unstage the Changes

Use:

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

or simply:

```bash
git reset HEAD~1
```

This removes the last commit while keeping the file modifications in the working directory.

Afterwards:

```bash
git status
```

will show:

```text
Changes not staged for commit:
    modified: index.php
```

So:

```text
Commit removed
      ↓
Changes kept
      ↓
Changes unstaged
```

This is one of the most useful ways to undo a local commit.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 11. Undo the Last Commit and Delete the Changes

If you want to completely destroy the last commit **and its changes**:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

This moves `HEAD` backward and makes the working directory match that commit.

```text
Before:

A → B → C
        ↑
       HEAD

After:

A → B
    ↑
   HEAD
```

Commit `C` is removed from the current branch.

&gt; **Warning**
&gt; `--hard` can destroy uncommitted work.

Never use it casually.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 12. Understanding `HEAD~1`

You will frequently see:

```bash
HEAD~1
```

It means:

&gt; The parent of the current commit.

For example:

```text
A → B → C
        ↑
       HEAD
```

Then:

```text
HEAD      = C
HEAD~1    = B
HEAD~2    = A
```

Therefore:

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

means:

&gt; Move the current branch one commit backward.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 13. Undo Multiple Local Commits

Suppose history is:

```text
A → B → C → D → E
              ↑
             HEAD
```

To remove the last three commits:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~3
```

Result:

```text
A → B
    ↑
   HEAD
```

Again, this rewrites local history.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 14. Undo a Commit but Keep Its Changes

There are three important reset modes:

| Command                    | Commit removed? | Changes kept? | Changes staged? |
| -------------------------- | --------------: | ------------: | --------------: |
| `git reset --soft HEAD~1`  |             Yes |           Yes |             Yes |
| `git reset --mixed HEAD~1` |             Yes |           Yes |              No |
| `git reset --hard HEAD~1`  |             Yes |            No |              No |

This is one of the most important Git concepts.

**Soft**

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

**Undo commit, keep changes staged.**

**Mixed**

```bash
git reset --mixed HEAD~1
```

**Undo commit, keep changes unstaged.**

**Hard**

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

**Undo commit and discard changes.**

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 15. Fix the Last Commit Instead of Undoing It

Sometimes you don't actually need to undo the commit.

Suppose you forgot to include:

```text
style.css
```

in the last commit.

Stage it:

```bash
git add style.css
```

Then modify the previous commit:

```bash
git commit --amend
```

Or:

```bash
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

`--no-edit` keeps the existing commit message.

So:

```bash
git add style.css
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

turns:

```text
Commit A
```

into an updated:

```text
Commit A'
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 16. Undo a Commit That Has Already Been Pushed

This is where things become more important.

Suppose:

```text
Local:
A → B → C

Remote:
A → B → C
```

You want to undo commit `C`.

If the commit has already been shared with others, the safest approach is usually:

```bash
git revert C
```

Git creates a new commit that reverses the changes introduced by `C`.

Result:

```text
A → B → C → D
             ↑
        revert C
```

Commit `D` effectively undoes `C`.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 17. `git revert` vs `git reset`

This distinction is extremely important.

| Situation                         | Recommended  |
| --------------------------------- | ------------ |
| Undo local commit                 | `git reset`  |
| Rewrite local history             | `git reset`  |
| Undo already-pushed/shared commit | `git revert` |
| Keep commit history unchanged     | `git revert` |

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Reset**

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

moves the branch backward.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-md-6
**Revert**

```bash
git revert HEAD
```

creates a new commit that reverses the previous commit.

For shared branches such as:

```text
main
production
```

`git revert` is generally safer than rewriting history.
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 18. Revert a Specific Older Commit

Suppose:

```text
A → B → C → D → E
```

You discover that `B` introduced a bug.

First find its hash:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

Example:

```text
e5a1234 Update documentation
d7b4321 Fix login
c8f1234 Update UI
b2a5678 Add authentication
a1c2345 Initial commit
```

Then:

```bash
git revert b2a5678
```

Git creates a new commit reversing the changes introduced by that commit.

The history becomes:

```text
A → B → C → D → E → F
                     ↑
               revert of B
```

The original commit remains in history.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 19. Undo Multiple Commits with Revert

You can revert multiple commits, but be careful about the order and whether the changes depend on each other.

For example:

```bash
git revert HEAD
```

undoes the latest commit.

You can also specify a range:

```bash
git revert OLDER_COMMIT..NEWER_COMMIT
```

Git will create revert commits for the specified commits.

If conflicts occur, resolve them and continue:

```bash
git add .
git revert --continue
```

To cancel the operation:

```bash
git revert --abort
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 20. Undo a Merge Commit

Merge commits require special handling.

Suppose:

```text
A → B → C
     \     \
      D → E → M
```

`M` is a merge commit.

To revert it:

```bash
git revert -m 1 <merge-commit>
```

The `-m 1` tells Git which parent should be treated as the mainline.

For example:

```bash
git revert -m 1 abc1234
```

This is particularly useful when a branch was merged into `main` and you want to undo the merge without rewriting shared history.
